\section{Migrate a New Keyword Without Capturing a Local Name}

Imagine an edition that newly reserves \texttt{stage}. A language-aware migration should preserve which declaration a use denotes, even when an innocent replacement name is already in scope. The following illustrative tool handles local variables only; exported names and macros can have additional compatibility contracts. The inputs are an old-edition source file, a parser and resolver for each edition, and a map of newly reserved spellings. Assume ordinary lexical scope and a way to correlate edited declaration and use nodes across the reparse. The output is a proposed source patch whose new-edition bindings match the original ones, or a diagnostic without an applied change.

\begin{verbatim}
migrate(source, old, new):
  tree = resolve(parse(source, old))
  links = reference_to_declaration(tree)
  # Reserve existing spellings to avoid capture.
  taken = all_identifier_spellings(tree) union keywords(new)
  edits = empty list
  for local in newly_reserved_locals(tree, new):
    name = fresh_variant(local.name, taken)
    add name to taken
    # Edit identifier tokens linked to this declaration only.
    add renaming edits for local and its uses
  proposal = apply_edits(source, edits)
  checked = try_parse_and_resolve(proposal, new)
  if not same_links(checked, links): return NeedsReview
  return proposal
\end{verbatim}

For \verb|let stage = 4; let stage_ = 9; print(stage, "stage")| the tool skips \texttt{stage\_} and chooses \texttt{stage\_2}. The new interpretation prints \(4\) and the literal \texttt{stage}, just as the old one did. The second declaration remains \texttt{stage\_}; neither comments nor the string are changed. Renaming only the declaration would leave the call with a reserved token, and renaming all occurrences of the substring would also alter the input printed by the program.

The binding arcs in Figure~\ref{fig:visual-190} distinguish the renamed use from an identical spelling inside a string.

% Explanatory visual
